  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">USAID - Forestry and Biodiversity Support Activity (FABS)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(USAID Central Africa Regional):  USAID will use FY 2022 Biodiversity and Sustainable Landscapes funds to address threats to biodiversity conservation and sound forest management in the Congo Basin. USAID&apos;s Forestry and Biodiversity Support activity will build the capacity of targeted ministries and regional platforms to understand and address local threats to the environment. It will include a small grants component, catalyze private sector investment in &quot;green enterprises,&quot; develop civil society organizations&apos; capacity to implement their own solutions and advocate for greater accountability of government, build capacity of government agencies to formulate and implement policies, and ensure lessons learned are institutionalized within Central Africa. The activity will integrate gender and vulnerable peoples&apos; considerations into grants, capacity-building, policy analysis, and advocacy...This activity will contribute to advancing the U.S. government priority of regional stability in Central Africa by maintaining the ecological integrity of humid forests of the Congo Basin. It contributes to Joint Strategic Plan Strategic Objective 2.2: (Promote healthy, educated and productive populations in partner countries to drive inclusive and sustainable development, open new markets and support U.S. prosperity and security objectives) by protecting natural resources critical to long term growth, food, and water security. The activity will build capacity of regional governments to meet Nationally Determined Contributions under the Paris Climate Agreement and access global climate financing through the Green Climate Fund and Central African Forest Initiative, while catalyzing private sector investment in low emission development. This activity will contribute to combating wildlife trafficking by strengthening the capacity of law enforcement agencies and empowering civil society organizations to hold them responsible for implementing wildlife laws..</narrative>
  </description>
